#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# identity_matrix

#% text
# `identity_matrix(n)` gives the `n` x `n` identity matrix.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The entries are exact: 1 on the diagonal and 0 elsewhere.

#% item
# `identity_matrix(0)` is the empty list.

#% item
# `n` must be a nonnegative integer; anything else gives a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
identity_matrix(3)
#% output
#= [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]]

#% cell
identity_matrix(1)
#% output
#= [[1]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The empty matrix:

#% cell
identity_matrix(0)
#% output
#= []

#% text
# A multiple of the identity:

#% cell
3*identity_matrix(2)
#% output
#= [[3, 0], [0, 3]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The characteristic polynomial of a matrix:

#% cell
det([[1, 2], [3, 4]] - x*identity_matrix(2))
#% output
#= -2 + -5*x + x^2

#% text
# The inverse as the solution of a system with the identity on the right:

#% cell
linear_solve([[2, 1], [7, 3]], identity_matrix(2))
#% output
#= [[-3, 1], [7, -2]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The identity leaves a matrix unchanged:

#% cell
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]; dot(identity_matrix(2), m) == m
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Its determinant is 1 and its trace is `n`:

#% cell
[det(identity_matrix(10)), trace(identity_matrix(10))]
#% output
#= [1, 10]

#% text
# It is the diagonal matrix of ones:

#% cell
diagonal_matrix([1, 1, 1]) == identity_matrix(3)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# All its eigenvalues are 1:

#% cell
eigenvalues(identity_matrix(4))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 1, 1]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The size must be a nonnegative integer:

#% cell
identity_matrix(-1)
#% output
#! failure(argument, identity_matrix, 1)

#% cell
identity_matrix(2.0)
#% output
#! failure(argument, identity_matrix, 1)

#% text
# A list of sizes for a rectangular matrix is not supported:

#% cell
identity_matrix([2, 3])
#% output
#! failure(argument, identity_matrix, 1)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `diagonal_matrix`, `dot`, `inverse`, `det`, `trace`
